    <string name="help_frame_generation">Shows extra pictures between the frames a game draws, so games that run at a low frame rate (often 20 or 30 fps) look smoother. The game itself is not changed or sped up.\n\n• Off: normal drawing.\n• Blend: cross-fades the last two game frames. Cheap, but moving things look a little ghosted.\n• Motion: finds how each part of the picture moved and moves it along before blending. Smoother and sharper; uses more CPU.\n• Motion HQ: like Motion, measured finer, to a quarter of a pixel, and following faster movement. The smoothest; uses the most CPU.\n\nIt adds about one game frame of delay to the controls. It needs the graphics mode HW acceleration (OpenGL ES).</string>
    <string name="help_frame_generation_fps">How many pictures per second frame generation shows.\n\n• Screen rate: one on every refresh of the screen (usually 60).\n• 60, 90, 120 fps: a fixed rate. 90 and 120 also ask Android to run the screen that fast, on phones whose screens can; otherwise the screen\'s own rate is the limit.\n\nA rate that does not divide the screen\'s rate (90 on a 120 Hz screen) is shown as evenly as the screen allows.</string>
    <string name="help_frame_generation_multi_core">Shares the motion search of Motion and Motion HQ between up to four CPU cores. The pictures are exactly the same, only worked out two to three times sooner, which lowers the added delay and helps Motion HQ keep up on slower phones.\n\nTurn it off to save battery. It has no effect with GPU search.</string>
    <string name="help_frame_generation_clean_edges">Where a moving object covers or uncovers the background, that part of the picture exists in only one of the two game frames, so blending them leaves a ghost or halo around the object. With this on, the motion is also searched backwards, and those parts are taken from the one frame where they can be seen.\n\nFewer halos around moving objects, but it doubles the CPU used for the motion search, and a wrong guess can show as a small sharp patch instead of a soft ghost.</string>
    <string name="help_frame_generation_gpu">Searches the motion with the graphics chip instead of the CPU, the same way as Motion HQ. It frees the CPU and removes the delay the CPU search adds.\n\nExperimental: on weak or older GPUs games may run slower with it. If a phone\'s GPU can not run it, the CPU searches instead.</string>
    <string name="help_frame_generation_steady">Gives each part of the picture the motion it had in the last frame as a first guess, because things in games usually keep moving the same way. This reduces shimmer in slow pans and on flat or repeating areas (sky, water, road markings), and follows steady movement that is too fast for the normal search.\n\nIf something turns or stops, the normal search takes over. On pictures made only of repeating patterns it can keep a wrong motion for a while, which shows as broken-up patches; that is why it is off by default.</string>
    <string name="help_frame_generation_low_latency">Shows each game frame as soon as the game draws it, then keeps moving the picture the way it was moving until the next frame comes. Normally a new frame is shown only after the pictures leading up to it, which adds about one game frame of delay (30 to 40 ms at 30 fps); with this on the controls respond almost as fast as with frame generation off.\n\nThe pictures between frames are a guess about where things are going. When something stops, turns or speeds up, the picture goes a little too far and is corrected when the next frame comes, and the edges of moving objects can smear a little. Best for fast action and racing games. It does not work with Blend, and Clean edges has no effect with it.</string>
    <string name="help_frame_generation_even_pacing">Places each extra picture for the moment the screen shows it, instead of for the moment drawing happened to start. Drawing starts a few milliseconds after each screen refresh, by a different amount every time, so without this, moving things advance by slightly uneven steps, which shows as a fine wobble in fast, steady movement (racing, scrolling, camera turns).\n\nIt also tells Android when each picture is due, so a picture that comes too late is skipped instead of being shown a refresh late.\n\nGames finish their frames a few milliseconds early or late too; with this on the pictures follow the game\'s rhythm instead of each frame\'s exact moment, which evens that out for about 4 ms more delay.\n\nIt costs almost no CPU and helps most on busy phones and at 90 or 120 fps. It can not fix a game that drops or holds frames.</string>
    <string name="help_render_3d">For games that use the Mascot Capsule 3D engine: draws the 3D scene at 2x or 3x the game\'s size and shrinks it down, so polygon edges are smooth instead of jagged. The game\'s picture stays the same size, so it smooths edges but does not add detail. 3x draws nine times as many pixels.\n\nIt has no effect on other games (M3G games, or games that draw their 3D themselves).</string>
